<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/1st-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Philippi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/42-bce">42 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberators-civil-war">Liberators' civil war</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Philippi was fought in October 42 BCE near the city of Philippi in eastern Macedonia. The forces of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-triumvirate">Second Triumvirate</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Mark Antony</a> and Octavian defeated the army of Brutus and Cassius, assassins of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a>, in two engagements, after which both killed themselves. The victory ended the republican cause and left the triumvirs masters of the Roman world.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-philippi.jpg" alt="Battle of Philippi" width="320" height="415" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marsyas · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Philippi_location.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the assassination of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> in 44 BCE, Brutus and Cassius, known as the Liberators, left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and took control of the eastern provinces from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and the allied eastern kingdoms. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> the three main Caesarian leaders, Antony, Octavian and Lepidus, established the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-triumvirate">Second Triumvirate</a> and crushed the Senate's opposition. They declared a civil war ostensibly to avenge Caesar, though the underlying cause was a long conflict between the Optimates and the Populares.</p><p>Lepidus stayed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> while Antony and Octavian moved to northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> with 28 legions, sending an advance force of eight legions along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/via-egnatia">Via Egnatia</a>. The Liberators, with the Republican admiral Gnaeus Domitius Ahenobarbus commanding 130 ships, wished to avoid a decisive battle and to use their naval superiority to cut the triumvirs' supplies from Italy. They took a fortified position about 3.5 kilometres west of Philippi, Brutus to the north of the Via Egnatia and Cassius to the south.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Each side probably had only 40,000 to 50,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">legionaries</a>. After Antony cut a causeway through the southern marshes, Cassius built a transverse wall against him, and a general battle began on 3 October 42 BCE. Brutus's troops attacked without waiting for orders, routed Octavian's army and captured its camp, though Octavian himself was not found in his tent. To the south Antony stormed Cassius's fortifications. Unable to see what had happened on Brutus's wing, and believing the day lost, Cassius ordered his freedman Pindarus to kill him. Brutus called him the last of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>.</p><p>On the same day the Republican fleet destroyed a convoy of reinforcements led by Gnaeus Domitius Calvinus. Over the next three weeks Antony advanced south, forcing Brutus to extend his line. Brutus attacked on the afternoon of 23 October. After close combat his thinned centre gave way, the triumvirs turned on his flank and rear, and his army could not re-form. Brutus retreated into the hills with the equivalent of four legions and took his own life.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Antony covered Brutus's body with a purple garment, according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a>. Many young aristocrats died in the battle or by suicide, among them Marcus Porcius Cato, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cato-the-younger">Cato the Younger</a>, and the father of Livia. About 14,000 survivors were enrolled in the triumvirs' army, and some veterans settled at Philippi, which became the Roman colony Colonia Victrix Philippensium. Antony stayed in the East and Octavian returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> to find land for the veterans. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sextus-pompey">Sextus Pompey</a> still held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, but Republican resistance had been crushed.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Philippi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Second Triumvirate of Mark Antony, Octavian and Lepidus defeated the assassins at <b>Philippi</b> in 42 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-republic">Roman Republic · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
